  "worked_solution": "To make our estimation, we proceed as follows:\n\n- We start at $y=30$ on the vertical axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/26ba10258a8794a97dfc5e61c8160b342ed6890f7a9af40c1e21ac814c779188.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/e50d79adb9803f6509314cf3512fcd388ccc78b287d6653b9e318ce54ce45022.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the horizontal axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/a9bd5a7973ecb2e88c328d0943d4bfebe9862b2cea1abbd0b3fa3665fa177167.png)\n\nTherefore, a chess player that wins $30$ games spends around $5$ hours training per day.",
